constexpr const char* kAppVersion     = "1.7.22";  // v1.7.22: adaptive tiers actually compiled and wired (graceful degradation pauses OCR/extraction under RAM pressure, semantic search stays on and throttles, first-launch shows the detected tier, Stats hosts a live health dashboard). v1.7.21: the two async pipelines were cut out of the 6,700-line MainWindow god-object — the exact place where every serious historical bug hid, because (as the review put it) "UI and async pipelines are untested ... logic tests can't catch wiring bugs". The extraction session machine (todo gathering, session cap, the 200 ms driver tick, the per-file pool hand-off, the DB accounting continuation, cancel, session generations, OCR accounting) now lives in ExtractionController, and the AI-embedding backfill (stale-vector invalidation, the two-phase document/chunk queue with chaining, the rebuild purge chain, the all-fail deadlock guard, the backlog counters) lives in EmbeddingController — both QtCore-only, zero UI dependencies, every resource constructed EAGERLY in the constructor and audited at startup by a wiring check that would have caught the "OCR pool declared but never constructed" bug on day one. MainWindow reports what it no longer owns and shrinks by ~1,100 lines; the controllers report upward through signals, so every wire between pipeline and UI is visible in one connect block. And the wiring is now TESTED: tests/tst_Wiring.cpp constructs the real controllers headless against a temp database and drives them end-to-end — construction contract, an unwired worker is detected instead of silently idling, the once-only extraction contract (each file extracted exactly once even when extraction is slower than the tick — the re-entrancy class), cancel/database-reset late-result drops, OCR session accounting, the backfill drain through an injected IEmbeddingService fake, the deadlock guard, the rebuild purge chain and the backlog counters. Writing the second-session regression test caught a REAL shipped bug of exactly that class: the v1.7.20 watcher continuation was connected once and captured the FIRST extraction session's state, so every later session's results failed the generation check and were silently dropped while the tick re-extracted the first file forever with no database writes — the second Extract click (or any >200-file first-run re-arm) would spin. The controller now reads its CURRENT session, and the regression test proves it. v1.7.20: background work and searching are now fully separated - search, extraction and embedding each run on their OWN thread, so background work can no longer affect or freeze searching (the request: 'search, extraction and embedding all are seperate threads so that bg work will not affect or freeze searching'). Text extraction moved off the global thread pool onto a dedicated single-file pool with 16 MB stacks, and the UI thread no longer busy-waits on it: the old loop pumped processEvents for the whole extraction of every file, which (a) kept the interface semantically busy during each file and (b) pumped timer events - so any file slower than the 200 ms pacing re-entered the extraction tick and extracted the SAME file again, nested, on several threads at once, double-incrementing the position and skipping neighbours (a latent correctness bug that also wasted CPU); results now land through a QFutureWatcher continuation, a session generation guard drops late results from cancelled sessions, and the destructor drains the pool. AI embedding moved onto its own dedicated pool too (model init + batch worker previously queued behind folder-scan tasks on the global pool), the semantic search scan keeps its v1.7.19 dedicated pool, and OCR already ran on its own workers - every background path now owns a thread. The splash animation was rebuilt for the persistent 'still stuck or glitch' report: as long as it was a Qt widget it could only repaint on the UI thread, and the UI thread is busy while the main window builds - so the animation now lives OFF that thread entirely: on Windows a native layered window on its own thread presents frames via UpdateLayeredWindow every 16 ms (the UI thread can stall for seconds and the sweep still runs at full frame rate), the fade-out animates the layered-window alpha in that same thread, the reveal order is unchanged (splash fades over the desktop, then the window shows), and the code-drawn artwork is one shared renderer used by both the native path and the old Qt widget (kept as a fallback if the native path fails). On top: the database (open + schema migrate) is pre-opened on a background thread while the splash plays, so the window constructor no longer blocks on sqlite at all and the main window typically appears sooner. v1.7.19: the AI result itself got fast - "if ai result is taking too much time means, what is the use?" Three hidden costs were found and removed from the semantic scan that v1.7.18 moved off the UI thread. (1) The all-chunks scan paginated with LIMIT 500 OFFSET k: SQLite must walk and discard every row before each batch, so a 100k-chunk library paid tens of millions of wasted row visits PER SEARCH (O(N^2)); now ONE prepared statement streams a rowid-range cursor (chunk_id is the table's INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, so the seek is a B-tree lookup + sequential read) and the table is walked exactly once regardless of size. (2) Per-row waste: every scanned chunk heap-allocated a fresh 384-float vector, re-converted the path/filename strings for every row of a file already seen, and ran a 3-accumulator cosine that recomputed the query norm (identical for every row!) and each row's norm (recomputed on every search) plus two sqrt/divisions; stored vectors are L2-normalized at embed time so cosine(a,b) == dot(a,b) - the scans now run a 4-way unrolled single-accumulator dot kernel (3x fewer FLOPs, auto-vectorizes under MSVC /O2, no sqrt), reuse one scratch buffer, and convert path strings once per file. (3) The semantic scan ran on the GLOBAL thread pool shared with per-file extraction tasks and the batch-embedding worker, so while a big folder was being extracted the AI scan waited SECONDS in the queue before it even started - it now runs on a dedicated single-thread pool and is immune to that starvation. On top: a superseded query's scan is cancelled cooperatively (an atomic flag checked per batch, threaded through BgeEmbeddingDb/BgeService/HybridSearchEngine) instead of running to completion while the next query waits behind the mutex, and query embeddings are LRU-cached (32 entries, keyed by the prefixed query text) so repeat and refined searches skip ONNX inference entirely. Net effect: keyword results stay instant, the AI merge lands in tens to hundreds of milliseconds instead of seconds. v1.7.18: search is fast again - the whole search (FTS + BGE query embedding + chunk/document cosine scans + rendering) used to run synchronously on the UI thread and froze input for seconds on real libraries (user-measured 3.6 s, "it defeat the purpose of this app"); now the keyword results appear immediately (typically tens of ms) while the semantic scan runs on a background thread and merges in when it lands - the status line honestly says "AI ranking in background...", AI additions still append after your keyword order (it never re-ranks), and a superseded query's stale AI result is discarded by a generation counter; the destructor joins the worker, the same lifetime contract as the BGE init future. The splash intro was re-smoothed ("still splash screen is bug"): the previous fade-in was itself a glitch - a main-thread animation stuttering under the busy constructor (opacity 0.2, freeze, 0.9) - so the splash now appears at full opacity in one crisp step, and the reveal order is fixed: the splash fades out over the desktop first (idle event loop, genuinely smooth 200 ms) and only then does the main window show, so nothing ever pops in behind the half-transparent card; the startup application palette is now theme-aware, which kills the light first-paint flash dark-mode users saw at the reveal. Tooltips: text and fitting fixed - the text used to sit 1 px from the card border (no inner padding) and QLabel sizeHint/heightForWidth measurement with word wrap is unreliable; sizes are now measured directly with QFontMetrics (multi-line aware, wrapped at 420 px, +2 px safety), the card gained real inner padding (10 x 7 px), and the tip is pinned to plain text so a "<" in a filename can never be misread as HTML. v1.7.17: the polish pass for everything you touch in the first ten seconds. The intro splash no longer glitches: it FADES in over 160 ms and FADES out over 200 ms when the main window is ready (windowOpacity is compositor work, so the transition stays smooth even while the CPU builds the window), and every animation tick now repaints only the strip that actually changes - progress bar + status caption - instead of the whole translucent 540x340 card 60 times a second, which was the single biggest startup paint load and the reason frames dropped and the sweep jumped. Closing the window now asks first: a confirm dialog states that closing is SAFE (the library is saved; indexing, extraction and embedding resume automatically next launch), honestly lists any work still running (folder scan / extraction / AI embedding / OCR), and carries a "Dont ask again" switch that persists immediately. On the very first launch a welcome dialog appears: it offers "Add my first folder" as the primary action and explains the pipeline up front - indexing is fast and keyword search works right away, while extraction and AI embedding take time, so full AI search by meaning arrives as embedding completes (the Extracted / Embedded badges show that progress); it runs once (welcomeDone is persisted whichever button closes it). Tooltips were redesigned from scratch: the native square system tip is replaced app-wide by a custom ModernTooltip - a rounded glass card drawn with the exact theme tooltip tokens (bg/text/border), hairline border, layered soft shadow, 130 ms fade-and-settle entrance, edge-aware positioning that flips near screen borders, and native-identical hide behavior (leave, press, wheel, moving to another widget, app deactivation). Widgets with no tooltip text keep the native path, so menu action tooltips still work. Dialog buttons gained a two-tier hierarchy: accent fill for the primary action, neutral surface for Cancel/Later (secondaryBtn). v1.7.16: rotated scans are finally searchable end-to-end. A scanned PDF can carry a text layer that the SCANNER's own (bad) OCR wrote into the file at scan time - on a rotated page that layer is punctuation-soup letter fragments ("AVAA'IIVH NHAISAAA HINOS ;; i g !c ..."), and the garbage classifier never even examined it (its word-sanity rules only judged letter-dominant text, and this junk is barely 53% letters), so the junk was indexed as real content: searching the file NAME worked, but "memorandum" or "pay fixation" found nothing, and the AI matched junk vectors into unrelated results. The classifier gained a fragment-soup gate - punctuation share, mean token length and long-word share, triple-redundant structural evidence with thresholds measured against real invoices, bank tables and German/French prose - and a ONE-TIME audit re-judges every already-stored text at first launch: poisoned files lose their text, their index row and their stale AI vectors, and are re-extracted automatically, which now routes them to OCR. The OCR auto-orientation verdict itself also grew up: it used to ask "how many characters came back" (a dense sideways page yields 1234 junk-run points vs 1399 upright - indistinguishable by volume), and now asks "did we read real WORDS" - the share of recognized tokens that are common words is near zero for sideways fragments and 0.2-0.4 for upright text, so the orientation that reads language always wins, and a correctly-orientated short page can no longer lose to a dense wall of junk runs. v1.7.15: semantic search fixed at the root — the AI now embeds the words you typed, not the query SYNTAX: the raw search-bar string (type:pdf, AND/OR/NOT, -draft, rail*) went into the embedding model verbatim, steering the query vector away from your actual words and even toward words you EXCLUDED; field filters, boolean operators, negations and wildcard stars are now stripped into ParsedQuery::semanticText (negated words are never embedded), pure-filter queries like "type:pdf" skip the AI scan entirely, and a Help section documents the AI toggle. The database schema layer was repaired: an old build stamped user_version = latest at the END of schema creation, so migrate() was a permanent no-op on every existing database — the EmbeddingChunks table was NEVER created for anyone (chunk-level semantic search silently did nothing) and the Extracted/Embedded stat chips froze at 0 because their one-statement count query referenced the missing table (chips now use two independent queries with an unknown-sentinel: one failing sub-count no longer blanks the other, and "Embedded" counts usable current-algorithm vectors only, so the number climbs visibly while AI indexing catches up). Embeddings now carry an algo_version stamp: rows produced by the old broken hash-fallback tokenizer are treated as missing and re-embedded automatically instead of polluting results forever (one-time re-embed of existing vectors on first launch). A legacy repair that reset the AI similarity threshold to 0.45 on EVERY launch is gone — it silently reverted anything you tuned in Settings. Deleting duplicate copies now shows a progress dialog ("Moving X of N" + the file name, cancellable — copies already moved stay moved, the rest stay in place, and the status summary discloses the early stop). v1.7.14: the duplicates cleanup grew into a real dialog — "Delete duplicates..." now offers a destination: Recycle Bin (restorable) or a folder you pick (files kept on disk, renamed on collision, size-verified copy+remove when a cross-volume rename fails, with an honest warning if the chosen folder sits inside an indexed one and would be re-indexed). The results header no longer fights itself: the sort dropdown yields its place while the delete action is armed (they could not both fit the 280-420 px pane, and touching the sort box used to disarm the delete button AND scramble the duplicate grouping). Folders added through the dash "Add Folder" button are now persisted into the same indexedDrives list Settings shows — previously they were invisible there AND excluded from startup watches, the hourly auto-scan, and the integrity pass. The top-right badge now shows the full pipeline: Indexed (searchable files), Extracted (text actually pulled out), Embedded (files the AI holds vectors for). Semantic search stopped padding results with noise: AI-only additions now need 0.60 cosine similarity (the old 0.45 bar sat inside BGE-small-en-v1.5's noise floor for unrelated text, so borderline-random files showed as "[AI match]" on nearly every query), and stale embeddings are invalidated — when a file's extracted text changes, its old vectors are deleted and re-embedded from the fresh text instead of being searched forever. v1.7.13: same-file self-pairs eliminated — one pathIdentityKey() now rules both the same-file collapse pass and a final pre-display guard (extended-length prefixes and UNC forms, mapped-drive spellings, dot segments, mixed separators, Windows case), so a single physical file can never again render as a "duplicate pair" (the "single file is showing as duplicates" report); the fresh-hash write-back now also refreshes the row's size and mtime (writing only the hash left drifted rows stale, re-hashed on every run, disagreeing with themselves); a stored hash is trusted only when the row carries a real mtime; a cancelled check leaves a persistent partial-results pill instead of an 8-second toast; NEW "Delete duplicate copies" button on duplicates results — keeps the NEWEST copy per group, moves the rest to the Recycle Bin (restorable, unlike unlink), re-validates every group against disk first (a group down to one survivor is never touched), purges the deleted rows, refreshes stats and re-runs the check so the pane shows the truth. v1.7.12: Detect Duplicates redesigned — compares file contents on demand instead of trusting stored hashes. The old finder was a pure DB query over the hash column, so it reported "no duplicates" whenever hashes did not happen to exist yet: unhashed rows (backfill 4000/launch lag) could never group, the v1.7.6 stale-hash gate DROPPED any row whose size/mtime moved since scanning (freshly copied duplicates, cloud-sync mtime churn, Save-As with identical content — all invisible), and hash computation off meant nothing would ever be found. The finder now pulls every indexable row (documents AND images), purges ghost rows, collapses rows pointing at the same physical file (mixed separators, dot segments, junctions, Windows case), pre-groups by exact on-disk byte size (free, kills 90%+ of I/O), fingerprints only collision groups (reusing a stored hash ONLY when it still matches the file on disk, else re-hashing with the same 64MB cap and writing the fresh hash back), groups by size+hash so the 64MB cap cannot make false pairs, shows a cancellable progress dialog, guards re-entrancy, orders results by group, and prints an honest empty message (files compared, fingerprints computed now, ghosts/collapsed/unreadable notes) that no longer blames a settings checkbox. The hash checkbox is reworded as a pre-compute speed switch. v1.7.11: production-readiness pass — every Settings control now takes effect (excluded-extensions gate all three ingest paths, live-monitor checkbox honored, worker-thread count made honest), the frameless Close button runs closeEvent (geometry/settings persist, indexing guard), BGE init + batch-embedding workers stop-and-join on exit (UAF windows closed), the never-constructed legacy Indexer subsystem (~515 lines) deleted, EXE/MSIX metadata synced, daily logs pruned after 14 days, and repo junk (.env, TEST-RESULTS*, worklog) untracked. On top of that branch, this release: stats "Total size" includes -wal/-shm, the stats worker's raw sqlite connection got busy_timeout, dead config fields (includedExtensions/thumbnailSize/lastBackupPath) and the never-emitted refreshRequested wire removed. v1.7.10: duplicates fixed for real — the Add-Folder scan (scanFolderFast) NEVER wrote the hash column, so every row it created had hash='' and the duplicate finder honestly-but-uselessly reported "no duplicates"; it now fingerprints files during the scan (same SHA-256 + 64MB cap as the hourly walk), duplicates cover documents AND images (identical scanned jpg/png pairs are real duplicates), the empty-result message states how many files still lack fingerprints instead of a bare "no", and the startup hash backfill is 4000/launch. Rotated PDFs/images OCR fixed — auto-orientation triggered only when upright OCR read <3 words, but sideways pages still yield 3+ junk fragments so the rotated passes never ran; replaced with a quality score (chars in 3+ letter runs, threshold 48) for PDFs AND images (images had no rotation retry at all), OCR rasters bounded to 2600px (Windows OCR accuracy cliff), failed rows get one honest retry per launch, and the OCR temp-file name collision between the two workers is fixed. New: Settings → Backup & Restore → "Remove Database (Reset)" wipes the index database (never user files), rebuilds schema and re-scans automatically; first run now extracts EVERYTHING automatically (200-file sessions, 3s re-arm) until the first full drain completes. v1.7.9: extraction + duplicates wiring repaired — the OCR worker pool was declared but NEVER constructed or enqueued (needs_ocr PDFs and ALL images were stranded and "Extract" said nothing to do); Extract now gathers needs_ocr + image rows and runs them on the pool; startup integrity pass requeues fake-done rows (pre-v1.7.3 scans stamped content_done without extracting) and backfills missing hashes so duplicates works; DocumentText always written (empty text = done). v1.7.8: startup un-blocked — the one-time non-document purge no longer runs before the window shows (on big legacy indexes it froze the splash for minutes, and killing the app mid-purge rolled the single transaction back so the NEXT launch froze too — the "stuck at splash" loop); purge now prepared-once + 500-row batched commits + event pumps, scheduled t+4.5 s; splash drops after 30 s so nothing can ever hide behind it; single-instance guard (second launch shows a visible message instead of fighting the running instance for the database). v1.7.7: ONE extension allowlist (kIndexableExtensions) now gates EVERY ingest path - hourly scan, FileWatcher, Add-Folder scan, full re-index - so .md/.txt/.csv/.rtf/.log and binary types never enter the index, and a one-time startup purge removes previously indexed non-documents; splash bar replaced by a smooth material sweep (no bounce stutter) with crossfaded captions and paced event pumps at every startup milestone; duplicates: document-only hash grouping + display-time existence re-verification (a file whose partner was moved/deleted can never render as a pair). v1.7.6: PDF preview use-after-free fixed (PDFium memory-buffer contract - the bytes now live with the document; flaky "error opening PDF" and garbled OCR/text gone); OCR auto-orients scans stored sideways (90/180/270 fallback via native PDFium rotation); duplicates: results list cleared when nothing found + stale-hash gate drops files whose content changed since scanning; theme wiring fixed (saved dark mode actually applies + toggle persists); splash matches the active theme (button-color accent). v1.7.5: AI fusion strictly additive; canonical duplicate identity; live edit indexing; PDF preview error overlay; splash animation under the event loop; real logo
